#. type: Title =
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "[.edit]#Command & Initiative#"
msgstr "[.edit]#Phase d'Ordres & Initiative#"

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:10
#, fuzzy
#| msgid ""
#| "Units [.bo5a]#and characters are# usually moved in the Command phase. A "
#| "unit usually requires orders before it will move but if the enemy is very "
#| "close, a unit is able to react directly to its presence. Otherwise, if a "
#| "unit receives no orders it remains where it is and awaits further "
#| "instructions.  [.bo5a]#Characters do move automatically and do so after "
#| "all unit movement is complete.#"
msgid ""
"Units [.bo5a]#and characters are# usually moved in the Command [.edit]#& "
"Initiative# phase. A unit usually requires orders before it will move but if "
"the enemy is very close, a unit is able to react directly to its presence. "
"Otherwise, if a unit receives no orders it remains where it is and awaits "
"further instructions.  [.bo5a]#Characters move automatically and do so after "
"all unit movement is complete.#"
msgstr ""
"Les unités [.bo5a]#et les personnages# sont majoritairement déplacées lors "
"de la phase d’Ordres. Une unité a généralement besoin d'ordres avant de se "
"déplacer, mais si l'ennemi est très proche, elle peut réagir directement à "
"sa présence. Sinon, tant qu’une unité ne reçoit pas d’ordre, elle reste là "
"où elle est et attend des instructions. [.bo5a]#Les personnages peuvent se "
"déplacer une fois tous les mouvements des unités terminés.#"

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:15
#, fuzzy
#| msgid ""
#| "The Command phase proceeds in the following sequence [.bo5a]#of sub-"
#| "phases. Of these, the Home Back sub-phase only applies to flying units "
#| "and so is not considered further in this section, see xref:flying-"
#| "units.adoc[]#."
msgid ""
"The Command [.edit]#& Initiative# phase proceeds in the following sequence "
"[.bo5a]#of sub-phases. Of these, the Home Back sub-phase only applies to "
"flying units and so is not considered further in this section, see "
"xref:flying-units.adoc[]#."
msgstr ""
"La phase d'Ordres se déroule suivant la liste [.bo5a]#de sous-phases "
"suivante. Parmi celles-ci, la sous-phase Revenir s'applique uniquement aux "
"unités volantes et n'est donc pas abordée en détail dans cette section. Voir "
"xref:flying-units.adoc[]#."

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:17
#, no-wrap
msgid "*<<initiative-movement>>*\n"
msgstr "*<<initiative-movement>>*\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:19
msgid "Any units moving by initiative do so."
msgstr "Les unités font leur mouvement par initiative."

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "*[.bo5a]#xref:flying-units.adoc#home-back[Home Back]#*\n"
msgstr "*[.bo5a]#xref:flying-units.adoc#reviennent[Revenir]#*\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:22
msgid "[.bo5a]#Flying units home back.#"
msgstr "[.bo5a]#Les unités volantes reviennent.#"

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:23
#, no-wrap
msgid "*<<orders>>*\n"
msgstr "*<<orders>>*\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:25
msgid "Orders are given to other units to move."
msgstr "Les ordres sont donnés aux unités à déplacer."

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:26
#, no-wrap
msgid "*[.bo5a]#<<character-movement>>#*\n"
msgstr "*[.bo5a]#<<character-movement>>#*\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:28
msgid "[.bo5a]#Characters are moved.#"
msgstr "[.bo5a]#Les personnages sont déplacés.#"

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:42
msgid ""
"A unit cannot move by means of initiative *and* orders in the same turn. If "
"a unit moves by initiative in the Initiative sub-phase, it *cannot* be given "
"orders in the Orders sub-phase."
msgstr ""
"Une unité ne peut pas se déplacer par initiative *et* par ordre au cours du "
"même tour. Si une unité se déplace par initiative lors de la sous-phase "
"d'Initiative, elle *ne peut pas* recevoir d'ordres lors de la sous-phase "
"d'Ordres."

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:48
msgid ""
"Note that all units moving by initiative must be moved before any orders are "
"issued. Once a character has issued an order, the Orders sub-phase has begun "
"and no further units can move by initiative."
msgstr ""
"Notez que toutes les unités se déplaçant par initiative doivent être "
"déplacées avant que le moindre ordre ait été donné. Une fois qu'un "
"personnage a donné un ordre, la sous-phase des Ordres commence et aucune "
"unité ne peut plus se déplacer par initiative."

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:52
#, fuzzy
#| msgid ""
#| "The distances moved by each type of unit vary, and are given in the "
#| "Movement section."
msgid ""
"The distances moved by each type of unit vary, and are given in the "
"xref:movement.adoc#movement-distances[Movement section]."
msgstr ""
"Les distances parcourues par chaque type d'unité varient et sont indiquées "
"dans la section Mouvement."

#. type: Title ==
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "Initiative movement"
msgstr "Mouvement par initiative"

#. // Warmaster Original text from Command chapter:
#. Before we concern ourselves further with initiative
#. movement, we need to understand how ordered
#. movement works. For this reason, an explanation of
#. initiative movement and further rules for it are given in
#. the Movement section of the rulebook.
#.  Warmaster Original text from Movement chapter:
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:74
msgid ""
"The Initiative rule represents the ability of a unit’s commanding officer to "
"lead his troops to the attack or guide them away from danger. Once the enemy "
"is close, a regiment’s training and natural instincts pretty much determine "
"what happens next, regardless of what the General might prefer."
msgstr ""
"La règle d'Initiative représente la capacité du commandant d'une unité à "
"mener ses troupes à l'attaque ou à les éloigner du danger. Une fois l'ennemi "
"proche, l'entraînement et l'instinct du régiment déterminent en grande "
"partie la suite des événements, quelles que soient les préférences du "
"général."

#
#
#. // Warmaster Original text from Command chapter:
#. Units within 20cm of the enemy at the start of the
#. Command phase can use their initiative to move without
#. orders. They do not have to do so – it is up to the player
#. to decide whether to use initiative or to issue an order. If
#. the player requires any of his units to use their initiative,
#. they must do so *before* any orders are issued.
#. // Warmaster Original text from Movement chapter:
#. During the Initiative Movement section of the Command
#. phase, a unit can use its own initiative to either charge or
#. evade from the closest visible enemy unit within 20cm.
#. It does not have to do so, and can instead wait until the
#. Ordered Movement section of the Command phase
#. and attempt to move by means of an order as usual.
#. The choice is the player’s in most cases; exceptions are
#. covered in the army lists.
#. If two or more enemy units are equally close a player
#. may choose which to charge or evade.
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:106
msgid ""
"[.ancients]#During the Initiative sub-phase,# a unit can use its own "
"initiative to either xref:movement.adoc#moving-chargers[charge] or "
"xref:movement.adoc#the-path-of-evaders[evade] from the *closest visible "
"enemy unit* within 20cm (see xref:game-values.adoc#visibility-on-the-"
"battlefield[Visibility on the battlefield]).  [.edit]#A unit does not have "
"to use its initiative, it# can instead wait until the [.edit]#Orders sub-"
"phase# and attempt to move by means of an order.  The choice is the player’s "
"in most cases; exceptions are covered in the army lists."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:113
msgid ""
"A unit can only react to the closest visible enemy unit and not other units "
"even if they are within 20cm and visible. If two or more enemy units are "
"equally close and both are visible, the player can choose which unit to "
"react to."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:129
msgid ""
"A unit moving by initiative can move in one of two ways. It can either "
"*xref:movement.adoc#moving-chargers[charge]* the enemy unit it is reacting "
"to, assuming it is able to do so, or it can *xref:movement.adoc#the-path-of-"
"evaders[evade]* from the enemy unit it is reacting to. Charging will bring "
"the opposing units into close combat whilst evading is a deliberate move to "
"avoid the enemy."
msgstr ""

#.  Warmaster Original text from Movement chapter:
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:135
msgid ""
"If you want a unit to do something else, such as move around a flank or "
"attack an enemy other than the closest, then you will have to issue an "
"<<orders,order>> instead."
msgstr ""

#.  Warmaster Original text from Movement chapter:
#.  Removed: This simply means a line of sight can be drawn from any
#.  free edge of any stand in the unit to any stand of another
#.  unit.
#.  Removed: potentially
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:149
msgid ""
"Some units can see all round – they have 360 degree vision – and you will "
"find this indicated in the unit’s description (for example, Wolf Riders in "
"the Orc army).  This can enable such a unit to use its initiative to evade "
"from an enemy positioned to its side or rear. However, note that even a unit "
"that can see all round cannot charge an enemy unless a line of sight can be "
"drawn from a stand’s front edge."
msgstr ""

#.  Warmaster Original text from Movement chapter:
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:155
msgid ""
"Artillery cannot use their initiative to charge, although they can use it to "
"evade. The crews of artillery, such as cannons or stone throwers have no "
"natural inclination and little ability to fight in close combat."
msgstr ""

#.  Warmaster Original text from Movement chapter:
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:164
msgid ""
"Enemy infantry or artillery in fortified positions can be ignored when it "
"comes to using initiative – these units are considered either unable to move "
"or unlikely to do so. A player can choose to ignore such enemy units if he "
"wishes, in which case his own unit can use initiative to charge or evade the "
"closest non-fortified enemy within 20cm."
msgstr ""

#.  Warmaster Original text from Movement chapter:
#.  (NB Ancients is the opposite to this!)
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:177
msgid ""
"A unit can also ignore the presence of enemy units if they are divided by a "
"mutually impassable barrier so long as neither the unit itself nor the enemy "
"unit(s) can move round the barrier within a full pace move. For example, "
"cavalry cannot cross rivers, so two cavalry units divided by a river can "
"ignore each other for purposes of initiative.  A player can choose to ignore "
"such enemy units if he wishes, in which case his own unit can use initiative "
"to charge or evade the closest visible enemy unit within 20cm that is not "
"divided by the impassable barrier."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:184
msgid ""
"In certain circumstances, a charging unit can carry a supporting unit with "
"it. This is an exception in that it allows the supporting unit to "
"effectively move by initiative even though it is neither charging nor "
"evading.  See xref:movement.adoc#supporting-charges[Supporting charges]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:194
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sequence of movement"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:219
msgid ""
"During the Initiative sub-phase, units move one at a time in any sequence "
"the player wishes.  Initiative is determined as each unit begins its move "
"and *not* at the beginning of the sub-phase."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:232
msgid ""
"Note that because units move one at a time, it is possible for one unit’s "
"move to block the line of sight of another so that it is subsequently unable "
"to use initiative. It is also possible for one unit’s move to block a unit’s "
"line of sight to one or more enemies, therefore affecting which enemy unit "
"is both closest and visible.  [.bo5a]#On the other hand,# it is equally "
"possible for a unit’s move to open a new line of sight for another so that "
"this second unit is now able to use initiative where before it could not. "
"These are all things for inexperienced players to be wary of. See "
"<<initiative-charge-1>> and <<initiative-charge-2>>."
msgstr ""

#.  Warmaster Original diagram from Movement chapter:
#.  TODO: Low-priority diagram text
#. type: Block title
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:235
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unit A is within 20cm of Enemy 1 and can see – it uses Initiative to charge. Note unit B cannot see any enemy in this position."
msgstr ""

#.  Warmaster Original diagram from Movement chapter:
#.  TODO: Low-priority diagram text
#. type: Block title
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:240
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now unit A has moved unit B can see enemy 2, and can use its Initiative to charge – unit A’s move has opened a line of sight for unit B to charge."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:246
#, no-wrap
msgid "Charge or evade"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:253
msgid ""
"As explained above, there are two types of initiative move: charge and "
"evade.  A unit *cannot* use initiative to move in any other way. The rules "
"for xref:movement.adoc#moving-chargers[charges] and xref:movement.adoc#the-"
"path-of-evaders[evades] are described in the Movement section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:270
msgid ""
"Issuing orders is one of the most important features of Warmaster.  "
"[.bo5a]#Only characters can issue orders – this is their primary function in "
"the game.# This represents the process of relaying instructions by "
"messenger, or a local commander’s interpretation of orders either given by "
"signal or arranged before the battle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:290
msgid ""
"[.bo5a]#An order must be issued to a unit to enable it to move in the Orders "
"sub-phase.# Once a unit has been given an order, it is moved and can be "
"given a further order and moved again, up to a maximum of *three* times in "
"total.  This process is described in detail in the rules that follow."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:293
#, no-wrap
msgid "Giving an order"
msgstr "Donner un ordre"

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:301
msgid ""
"The player [.bo5a]#first nominates a unit that# he wishes to move "
"[.bo5a]#and indicates the character that will give the necessary order#.  He "
"must then take a dice test to determine if the order is received and acted "
"upon. This works in the following way:"
msgstr ""

#.  Spelling i.e.
#.  NB: Half-pace failed orders not in Ancients/Bo5A.
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:312
msgid ""
"Roll 2D6 (i.e., roll two dice and add the results to get a score of between "
"2 and 12). If the score is equal to or less than the character’s Command "
"value then the unit has received its order and can move full pace (see "
"xref::movement.adoc#movement-distances[Movement [.edit]#distances#]). If the "
"score is more than the character’s Command value then the unit has not "
"received its order.  [.edit]#If this is the unit’s first order then# it can "
"move only half pace. This represents unit’s commanders being able to act on "
"their own to some limited extent."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:319
#, no-wrap
msgid "Order sequence"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:326
msgid ""
"Once a character has begun to issue orders, he must finish issuing all of "
"his orders before another character can start to do so. It is not permitted "
"for a character to issue an order, then a different character to issue one, "
"then the first character again."
msgstr ""

#
#. // WMR Removed as unnecessary.
#. .Command values
#. ****
#. This table shows the Command values for different
#. Generals. As you can see, there are three basic
#. values and some are better than others.
#. [cols="<,>",frame=none,grid=rows]
#. |===
#. |High Elf  |10
#. |Dwarf     |10
#. |Empire    |9
#. |Tomb King |9
#. |Chaos     |9
#. |Orc       |8
#. |===
#. ****
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:359
msgid ""
"Once a character has failed to give an order, he cannot issue any more "
"orders in that Command phase. Once the General has failed to give an order, "
"no further orders can be issued by any other characters in that Command "
"phase even if they have not done so already.  The character has run out of "
"time, the distance between him and the unit he is issuing orders to is too "
"great for his signals to be discerned, or the message may have been lost or "
"confused (perhaps the messenger has been caught up in nearby enemy action "
"and killed)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:364
#, no-wrap
msgid "Giving further orders"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:370
msgid ""
"When a unit is given an order, it moves to a new position.  Usually this "
"enables the unit to move 20cm if it is infantry or 30cm if cavalry, but some "
"troops move at different speeds as we shall see later."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:384
msgid ""
"Once a unit has moved, the character can give it a further order to move "
"again or he can attempt to give an order to a different unit. However, he is "
"not permitted to go back to a previously moved unit once he has moved "
"another unit. Nor is a different character permitted to give a unit an order "
"if that unit has already been given an order that turn, even if the order "
"was failed. This is an important rule so it is worth remembering right from "
"the start. If you want to move a unit several times, the character must "
"finish ordering it to move completely before he can try to issue an order to "
"a different unit. Note that a unit can be given up to a maximum of *three* "
"orders in succession. A unit failing to receive a *further* order *cannot* "
"move [.edit]#any further in this phase#."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:394
msgid ""
"The player continues issuing orders and moving his units until he no longer "
"wishes to move or until he can issue no more orders. It is possible to fail "
"to issue any orders at all during a Command phase, although this happens "
"rarely.  More commonly, a player might manage to move only one or two units "
"before a dice roll ends movement. This represents the confusion of battle, "
"the indecisiveness of unit leaders, occasional stupidity and all those other "
"annoying things that bedevil the process of waging war."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:399
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:518
#, no-wrap
msgid "Command penalties"
msgstr "Malus de Commandement"

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:408
msgid ""
"Circumstances can sometimes make it harder to issue an order. For example, a "
"unit might be a far distant speck on the horizon or it might be obscured "
"within dense terrain.  All of these things make it harder for the character "
"to identify what is going on and less likely that an effective order will be "
"formulated or received. To represent this, we apply the following penalties:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:414
#, no-wrap
msgid "Distance"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:421
msgid ""
"If the distance between the character and the unit you wish to move is more "
"than 20cm, the character suffers a Command penalty of −1 per full 20cm of "
"distance. When measuring distances between characters and units in this way, "
"measure the shortest distance between them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:431
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"|Distance to Unit |Command Penalty\n"
"\n"
"|Up to 20cm  |none\n"
"|Up to 40cm  |−1\n"
"|Up to 60cm  |−2\n"
"|Up to 80cm  |−3\n"
"|per +20cm   |minus a further 1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:436
#, no-wrap
msgid "Multiple orders"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:445
msgid ""
"If a unit has already been issued an order during the Command phase then "
"there is a Command penalty of −1 each time the character gives it a further "
"order. This accounts for fatigue and the limits of time, so a unit which has "
"already moved several times is harder to move again. This penalty is "
"cumulative, so a unit’s second order is at −1, its third order is at −2."
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:453
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"|Order  |Command Penalty\n"
"\n"
"|First  |none\n"
"|Second |−1\n"
"|Third  |−2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:458
#, no-wrap
msgid "Enemy close"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:467
msgid ""
"If the distance between the unit you wish to move and the closest enemy unit "
"is 20cm or less, there is a Command penalty of −1. Units close to the enemy "
"are naturally inclined to use their initiative to react, so it is harder to "
"give them a specific order.  [.bo5a]#This penalty applies even if the unit "
"is not visible (after all if you can’t see it, how do you know it isn’t "
"there?).#"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:474
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"|Closest Enemy  |Command Penalty\n"
"\n"
"|Up to 20cm     |−1\n"
"|More than 20cm |none\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:479
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/movement.adoc:997
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dense terrain"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:487
msgid ""
"If the unit you want to move is in dense terrain then there is a Command "
"penalty of −1. The penalty applies if at least one stand is even partially "
"within dense terrain.  Typical dense terrain features are woods, in and "
"around buildings, ruins and similar. See the "
"xref::movement.adoc#terrain[Movement section for more about dense terrain]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:493
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"|Dense Terrain  |Command Penalty\n"
"\n"
"|Within feature |−1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:504
msgid ""
"If the unit has lost one or more stands as casualties then there is a "
"Command penalty of −1 per stand lost.  Units which have suffered casualties "
"are harder to motivate than fresh units."
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:510
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"|Casualties  |Command Penalty\n"
"\n"
"|Each Stand  |−1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block = 4
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:531
msgid ""
"The General has a Command value of 9.  He has already moved his cavalry "
"forward once.  Now he wishes to move them again, hoping to capture a hill "
"that lies on the enemy’s flank. The cavalry are 65cm away and have already "
"moved once, which means a whopping −4 Command penalty (−3 for distance and "
"−1 for the second move). The player will have to roll 5 or less to give the "
"unit an order.  He knows this is unlikely but decides to take the risk "
"anyway as he considers the hill an important objective. He rolls 2D6 and "
"scores 4… success! The player moves the cavalry into their new position."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block = 4
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:537
msgid ""
"Next, he attempts to give orders to a unit of infantry 10cm away# [.wmr]#(no "
"penalty) and within a wood (−1 dense terrain penalty).# Although he only "
"needs 8 or less, this time he is unlucky and rolls a 10. The unit does not "
"move – the General has failed an order so the Orders sub-phase comes to an "
"end."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block * 4
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:546
msgid ""
"All Characters have +1 bonus to their Command value for their first command "
"of the first turn.  This represents a better state of organisation and "
"communication before the battle commences. Note that the Command value can "
"never exceed 10."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:552
#, no-wrap
msgid "Charge!"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:560
msgid ""
"A move which brings a unit into contact with the enemy is called a charge. A "
"charge does not imply that the unit concerned covers the whole distance at a "
"mad gallop but, nonetheless, we will use this word as a convenient and "
"dramatic term to describe a move into confrontation.  Units that failed to "
"receive an order cannot charge."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:565
msgid ""
"Once it has charged, a unit cannot receive any further orders that turn. It "
"is committed to battle and must fight in the ensuing Combat phase. See the "
"xref::combat.adoc[Combat phase section] for further explanation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:566
#, no-wrap
msgid "A unit of cavalry charging into combat with a unit of infantry"
msgstr ""

#.  Moved here from Movement chapter
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:583
msgid ""
"Characters move at the end of the Command phase after unit movement has been "
"completed. Characters never move with units during the Command phase, not "
"even if a character joined a unit in a previous turn or if such a unit "
"charges or evades using initiative. When units are repositioned during other "
"phases, during pursuit combat for example, characters which have joined "
"units are moved with them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:591
msgid ""
"Each character can move once and up to 60cm, or 100cm if flying. Characters "
"can always move at full pace and, unless mounted on a monstrous mount or "
"riding a chariot, always treat terrain as if they were infantry. No Command "
"test is required for a character to move. Any failed Order rolls made in the "
"Command phase do not affect [.edit]#a character’s# ability to move."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:601
msgid ""
"Due to their unique role in Warmaster, characters are treated differently to "
"other types of units. Character stands are essentially ‘tokens’ that are "
"considered to be ‘transparent’ on the battlefield. In other words, this "
"means they can move through units from their own side, and similarly, a unit "
"can move through its own side’s characters. All stands from either side can "
"see past and, if they are appropriately armed, can shoot through characters "
"as if they were not there."
msgstr ""

#.  End moved here
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:603
msgid "See the xref::characters.adoc[Generals, Wizards and Heroes section]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:608
#, no-wrap
msgid "Brigade orders"
msgstr "Ordres des Brigades"

#.  also Further Brigade Orders
#.  I think the Ancients/Bo5a text is a little clearer,
#.  but to avoid rewriting the entire rulebook I'm
#.  leaving the Warmaster text here as it's still OK.
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:624
msgid ""
"In order to save time, it is permitted for an order to be issued to up to "
"four adjacent units simultaneously. This means that fewer tests need to be "
"taken to issue orders to the entire army and ensures that units move at the "
"same time, maintaining a coherent battle plan. If you find this a bit "
"confusing, don’t worry. You can safely ignore the Brigade rules for your "
"first few games. Later on, once you’ve got the hang of other aspects of the "
"game, you can have a go at giving orders in this way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:633
msgid ""
"A brigade consists of up to four units arranged so that they touch, forming "
"a single body. Units comprising a brigade at the start of the ordered "
"movement part of the Command phase can be given a single order and are moved "
"together as a body. Once it has moved, the brigade can be given further "
"orders if required, potentially moving several times in just the same way as "
"individual units."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:644
msgid ""
"Bear in mind that it is not obligatory to move units as a brigade simply "
"because they happen to be touching at the start of the Command phase. A "
"brigade is not a formal division of the army but a convenient ad hoc "
"formation that can be changed from one turn to the next.  Units formed up "
"together can be given separate orders if you prefer, or two or three units "
"can be divided from a larger formation and treated as a completely separate "
"brigade. It is really up to you whether you choose to move touching units as "
"a brigade or not."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:646
#, no-wrap
msgid "These three units can be formed into a brigade."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:649
#, no-wrap
msgid "These four units can also be formed into a brigade. Each unit is arranged into a column that is three stands deep."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:670
msgid ""
"To give orders to a brigade, measure to the most distant unit in the brigade "
"and [.ancients]#apply the appropriate distance penalty. Also# apply the −1 "
"penalty if any units in the brigade are within 20cm of the enemy, in dense "
"terrain, if the brigade has moved before or if any units have lost "
"casualties. Don’t apply any penalties more than once (if two units are in "
"dense terrain for example) and in the case of casualties apply the penalty "
"for the unit which has lost the most stands. If your test is successful, "
"[.ancients]#then *all* the units have received an order, if the test is "
"failed, *all* the units have failed to receive an order# and the whole "
"brigade moves at half pace or doesn’t move [.edit]#further# depending on "
"whether it is a first or subsequent order (see xref::command.adoc#giving-an-"
"order[Giving an order])."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:675
msgid ""
"Assuming an order is successfully issued, the brigade can move. Except when "
"units wish to charge, brigades move as a body, with each unit remaining in "
"touch with the brigade as a whole."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:682
msgid ""
"Individual units in the brigade can change their relative positions but must "
"still form a brigade once its move is complete. Individual units in the "
"brigade can change formation as they move. No stand in any unit in the "
"brigade may move further than its permitted move distance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:693
msgid ""
"Conceivably, some units in a brigade might wish to charge the enemy whilst "
"others do not, as shown in <<f-brigade-orders,the following diagrams>>.  Any "
"units in a brigade that wish to charge do not have to remain in touch with "
"the rest of the brigade as a whole. Even though a single brigade order has "
"been issued, individual units can always charge. They do so individually, "
"one at a time, exactly as if they had been issued separate orders. Units "
"which do not charge still have to end their move touching."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:717
#, no-wrap
msgid "Further brigade orders"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:727
msgid ""
"Once a brigade has moved, the player may not want to move the whole brigade "
"again but might wish to move an individual unit or subset of touching units. "
"Alternatively, he may wish to divide the brigade into two and move it in two "
"different directions. To do this, the player must issue a separate order to "
"each unit or sub-brigade. The individual units or sub-sets will carry over "
"any Command penalty for a second or subsequent move."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:735
msgid ""
"Once a brigade has moved, the player must finish moving all the units in the "
"original brigade before he moves other units. If he divides a brigade into "
"two (say A and B), he must complete the movement of each subset in turn "
"before moving anything else (so he must finish moving group A, then group B "
"and then he can move other units in the army)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:739
msgid ""
"Brigades cannot use initiative as a body. Units using initiative must be "
"moved at the start of the Command phase in the usual way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/command.adoc:748
msgid ""
"During a battle, units may be moved into touch to form a new brigade. "
"However, a brigade cannot be formed and subsequently moved in the same "
"Command phase. Units must be in a brigade at the start of the "
"[.ancients]#Orders sub-phase# to move as a brigade.  [.ancients]#This means "
"it is necessary to arrange units so that they touch in one turn if they are "
"to be given a brigade order in the next.#"
msgstr ""
